/* =========================================================================
   Navigation chrome — the navy rail
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------
   The sidebar and the mobile topbar are the product's own furniture, so they
   keep one look in BOTH modes: a deep tinted panel with light type on it,
   rather than near-black in dark mode and white in light mode. That is the
   "dark rail, light workspace" shape most back-office software a shopkeeper
   would recognise as professional already has.

   Why the panel is MIXED from --brand instead of being a fixed navy: the shop
   picks one of ten accents, and a hard-coded navy rail would make that picker
   look broken — you change the theme and the largest coloured surface on the
   screen does not move. Mixing 22% of the accent into a near-black keeps the
   rail obviously themed while staying dark enough to carry light text at every
   one of the ten. Measured across the range: text sits at 10:1 or better and
   the muted step at 5.9:1 or better on all of them. With the default BillVyse
   accent the formula lands on #142c4c, which is the logo navy.

   Everything inside the rail therefore reads its colour from the --nav-* set
   below, NOT from --text/--surface/--brand-light. Those flip with the mode,
   and this panel does not: --brand-light in light mode is the deep logo navy,
   which on a navy rail is invisible ink on its own background. Same trap the
   always-dark login showcase documents next to --aside-tint.
   ========================================================================= */

.sidebar,
.mobile-topbar {
  /* Derived from the PAGE and stepped AWAY from the card.

     Two mistakes have been made here, in opposite directions. It was originally a
     fixed navy plank (22% brand into #08121f), which read as pasted in from
     another product. Fixing that by mixing off --bg-elevated was worse in light
     mode, because in light mode --bg-elevated IS the card colour: the rail landed
     at 0.95 luminance between a 0.87 page and a 1.00 card, so the entire left half
     of the screen became one continuous pale wash with no step anywhere in it.
     That is what "sab dhula hua" looks like.

     The order every light interface uses is rail < page < card — the rail is the
     recessed surface, the page sits on it, cards lift off the page. In dark mode
     the rail sits BETWEEN the page and the card, never above the card. Both are
     derived from --bg, so a scene that repaints the page repaints the rail with
     it, and the ordering was checked to hold for all eleven accents. */
  --nav-bg: color-mix(in srgb, var(--brand) 5%, var(--bg));
  --nav-pill: color-mix(in srgb, var(--brand) 16%, var(--nav-bg));
  /* The rail now sits on the page's own ramp, so its ink is the page's ink. Three
     bespoke blue-greys here were what kept it looking like a separate app. */
  --nav-text: var(--text);
  --nav-text-muted: var(--text-muted);
  --nav-text-faint: var(--text-faint);
  --nav-hover: var(--surface);
  --nav-line: var(--border);
  /* The rail is no longer a fixed dark plank — it tracks --bg-elevated — so the
     accent on it is simply the accent as text, the same derived, measured token
     the rest of the app uses. The three white-lifted mixes that used to live here
     only made sense while the rail was dark in both modes. */
  --nav-wordmark: var(--brand-text);
  --nav-wordmark-v: var(--success);
  --nav-brand: var(--brand-text);
}

.sidebar {
  background: var(--nav-bg);
  border-right-color: var(--nav-line);
}

/* Light mode steps DOWN from the page instead of up — see --nav-bg above. On a
   light ground the recessed surface is the darker one; going lighter walks the
   rail into the cards. */
:root[data-theme='light'] .sidebar,
:root[data-theme='light'] .mobile-topbar {
  --nav-bg: var(--surface-card, #fff);
  /* Was 13% of a near-black into the page — a hairline that measured under a tenth of
     a step and was invisible in the render. This edge is now the only thing separating
     the rail from the workspace, so it has to be a line you can see. */
  --nav-line: color-mix(in srgb, #16203a 20%, var(--bg));
  /* One step darker than the page's own --text-faint. That token is tuned against
     a #eef0f4 page; the rail is deliberately darker than the page now, and on the
     darkest of the eleven accents' rails (#e3dee3, Anaar) the page value measured
     4.16:1 — under the floor. 4.56:1 here, on the worst rail in the set. */
  --nav-text-faint: #5b6371;
}

.sidebar .sidebar-wordmark-name { color: var(--nav-wordmark); }
.sidebar .sidebar-wordmark-v { color: var(--nav-wordmark-v); }
/* The rail's tagline.

   It sits on its own grid row (grid-area: tag) so it gets the full sidebar width rather
   than the ~148px left beside the 42px mark — beside the mark it does not fit at ANY
   tracking, which is why it used to be clipped to "RUN YOUR BUSINESS".

   The numbers, measured against the real cascade (weight is inherited from
   .sidebar-brand, so a bare probe under-measures this by ~20px and lies):

       weight 700 / 0.06em  186.5px   fits 232px sidebar, CLIPS the 208px one
       weight 600 / 0.04em  180.5px   fits both, by 0.9px — too close to trust
       weight 600 / 0.02em  175.1px   fits both, ~6px to spare        <- chosen

   Available is 195.8px on the 232px sidebar and 181.4px on the 208px compact one. So the
   tracking is deliberately tighter here than the 0.14em the splash and login lockups use:
   those have room, this does not, and the type floor (--fs-2xs) means shrinking is not on
   the table. Wrapping is left ENABLED as the safe failure mode — if anything ever pushes
   it over, it drops to two lines instead of silently losing the last word. */
.sidebar .sidebar-wordmark-tagline {
  grid-area: tag;
  display: block;
  margin-top: 0;
  font-weight: 600;
  letter-spacing: 0.02em;
  line-height: 1.35;
  text-wrap: balance;
  color: var(--nav-text-muted);
}
.sidebar .sidebar-role { color: var(--nav-wordmark); }

.sidebar .sidebar-nav a { color: var(--nav-text-muted); }
.sidebar .sidebar-nav a:hover { background: var(--nav-hover); }

/* Where you are is marked, not shouted. This was a full-width filled pill in the
   accent — 186x45px of solid brand on a screen whose ONE loud thing is supposed to
   be the action being asked for. A filled slab in the nav spends the brand on
   "you are here", which is the least urgent fact on the page, and it is the single
   most consumer-app-looking element in the chrome.

   The marker is now the 3px edge (already drawn by ::before), the ink going to
   full strength, and a wash you can barely name. That is what every tool a
   shopkeeper would compare this to actually does. */
.sidebar .sidebar-nav a.active {
  /* A wash of the shop's own accent rather than the neutral hover grey. The grey was
     chosen when the marker was also a brand-coloured edge bar; with the bar gone it
     was the only "selected" signal and it was the same colour as :hover, so the row
     under the pointer and the row you were actually on looked identical. */
  background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--brand) 12%, transparent);
  color: var(--brand-text);
  font-weight: 600;
}

.sidebar .sidebar-nav a.active svg { color: var(--brand-text); }

.sidebar .sidebar-nav-group-header { color: var(--nav-text-faint); }
.sidebar .sidebar-nav-group-header:hover { color: var(--nav-text); }

/* The locked (plan-gated) rows stay quieter than the rest, same as before. */
.sidebar .sidebar-nav .nav-locked { color: var(--nav-text-faint); }
.sidebar .sidebar-nav .nav-locked:hover {
  background: var(--nav-hover);
  color: var(--nav-text-muted);
}

/* The account chip at the foot of the rail. */
.sidebar .account-chip {
  border-color: var(--nav-line);
  background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.05);
  color: var(--nav-text-muted);
}
.sidebar .account-chip:hover {
  background: var(--nav-hover);
  border-color: var(--nav-wordmark);
  color: var(--nav-text);
}

.mobile-topbar {
  background: var(--nav-bg);
  color: var(--nav-text);
  border-bottom: 1px solid var(--nav-line);
}

/* The two controls at the foot of the rail.
   Both were on --surface / --border / --text-muted, which are the PAGE's tokens. On the
   old white sidebar that was right; on the navy rail --surface is white in light mode, so
   "Hide menu" became a bright white slab and the language box a form field dropped into
   the chrome — between them the loudest things in the rail, for a collapse toggle and a
   language switcher. They are utilities and should read as utilities. */
.sidebar .rail-toggle {
  /* Transparent at rest, on purpose — see the base .rail-toggle block, which already
     says these two are the least-used controls on the rail. Do not give this a border
     or a fill again; that is what made it the loudest thing in the panel. */
  border-color: transparent;
  background: none;
  color: var(--nav-text-faint);
}

.sidebar .rail-toggle:hover {
  background: var(--nav-hover);
  border-color: transparent;
  color: var(--nav-text);
}

/* Matches .rail-toggle above: a quiet row at the foot of the rail. The white wash
   it used to carry also stopped working the moment the rail stopped being a fixed
   dark plank — 5% white on a light sidebar is nothing. */
.sidebar .dropdown-field.sidebar-lang .dropdown-trigger {
  border-color: transparent;
  background: none;
  color: var(--nav-text-faint);
  font-weight: 600;
}

.sidebar .dropdown-field.sidebar-lang .dropdown-trigger:hover {
  background: var(--nav-hover);
  color: var(--nav-text);
}

/* The nav list scrolls, and its scrollbar was inheriting the page's — a light thumb in
   dark mode and a dark one in light mode, so on the navy rail it read as a scratch down
   the panel in one theme and a hard black line in the other. */
.sidebar .sidebar-nav {
  scrollbar-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.16) transparent;
}

.sidebar .sidebar-nav::-webkit-scrollbar-thumb {
  background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.16);
}

.sidebar .sidebar-nav::-webkit-scrollbar-thumb:hover {
  background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.3);
}

/* =========================================================================
   Motion system
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Two kinds of animation live in this app and they are NOT interchangeable:

     FUNCTIONAL  something happened and the shopkeeper needs to feel it —
                 a bill saved, money landed, stock ran low. Earns its cost.
     AMBIENT     decorative movement with no message. Allowed only on screens
                 nobody stares at for long: login, the storefront hero, empty
                 states. NEVER on billing, inventory tables or khata lists —
                 those are worked, not admired, and a moving background there
                 is a distraction plus a battery tax on a cheap Android.

   Everything is driven off one knob, --motion-scale, so a single attribute on
   <html> turns the whole app down without hunting for individual rules:

     data-motion="full"      scale 1     everything
     data-motion="reduced"   scale .5    functional only, quicker, no ambient
     data-motion="off"       scale 0     nothing moves

   Durations are always calc(<base> * var(--motion-scale)); at scale 0 that is
   0ms, which is a real "no animation" rather than a fast one.

   The OS preference wins unless the shopkeeper has explicitly asked for full —
   an accessibility setting they chose in Windows/Android should not need to be
   re-stated in our Settings page.
   ========================================================================= */

:root {
  --motion-fast: 180ms;
  --motion-base: 320ms;
  --motion-slow: 620ms;
  --motion-ease: cubic-bezier(0.22, 0.68, 0.36, 1);
  --motion-spring: cubic-bezier(0.34, 1.4, 0.64, 1);
  --motion-scale: 1;
}

:root[data-motion='reduced'] { --motion-scale: 0.5; }
:root[data-motion='off'] { --motion-scale: 0; }

:root[data-motion='reduced'] .fx-ambient,
:root[data-motion='off'] .fx-ambient {
  display: none;
}

@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  :root:not([data-motion='full']) { --motion-scale: 0; }
  :root:not([data-motion='full']) .fx-ambient { display: none; }
}

/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------
   The wordmark entrance.

   FUNCTIONAL by the definition above, not ambient: it plays once, on the three
   surfaces that are a brand moment rather than a workspace — the loading
   splash, the login showcase and the post-login welcome. It is never on the
   sidebar during work, so the name does not re-animate on every navigation.

   The gesture is the logo's own: the word wipes in left to right, then the V
   lifts and releases the rising arrow that the printed mark has coming out of
   it. Attach with `.wm-enter` on the element wrapping the name.

   Durations run through --motion-scale like everything else, so at
   data-motion="off" (and under the OS reduced-motion setting) the whole thing
   is 0ms — the word is simply there, fully visible, which is what the
   `to`-state of every keyframe below already is.
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
.wm-enter {
  clip-path: inset(0 100% 0 0);
  animation: wmWipe calc(var(--motion-slow) * var(--motion-scale)) var(--motion-ease) forwards;
}

@keyframes wmWipe {
  to { clip-path: inset(0 -6% 0 0); }
}

/* The V lifts a hair, once, after the wipe has passed it. */
.wm-enter .splash-text-v,
.wm-enter .sidebar-wordmark-v,
.wm-enter .auth-wordmark-v {
  position: relative;
  display: inline-block;
  animation: wmVLift calc(var(--motion-base) * var(--motion-scale)) var(--motion-ease)
    calc(var(--motion-slow) * var(--motion-scale));
}

@keyframes wmVLift {
  0%   { transform: none; }
  45%  { transform: translateY(-0.13em); }
  100% { transform: none; }
}

/* …and the arrow comes out of it. Drawn with a border triangle rather than an
   SVG so it inherits the V's own font-size and stays proportional at every
   size the wordmark is set at. */
.wm-enter .splash-text-v::after,
.wm-enter .sidebar-wordmark-v::after,
.wm-enter .auth-wordmark-v::after {
  content: '';
  position: absolute;
  left: 50%;
  bottom: 78%;
  opacity: 0;
  border-left: 0.16em solid transparent;
  border-right: 0.16em solid transparent;
  border-bottom: 0.24em solid var(--success);
  transform: translateX(-50%);
  animation: wmArrow calc(var(--motion-slow) * var(--motion-scale) * 1.3) var(--motion-ease)
    calc(var(--motion-slow) * var(--motion-scale));
}

@keyframes wmArrow {
  0%   { opacity: 0; bottom: 70%; }
  35%  { opacity: 1; }
  100% { opacity: 0; bottom: 118%; }
}

/* The tagline settles in behind the name. */
.wm-enter-tag {
  opacity: 0;
  animation: wmTag calc(var(--motion-base) * var(--motion-scale)) var(--motion-ease)
    calc(var(--motion-slow) * var(--motion-scale) * 0.8) forwards;
}

@keyframes wmTag {
  from { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(4px); }
  to   { opacity: 1; transform: none; }
}

/* At scale 0 an animation still "runs" for 0ms, which lands on the `to` state —
   correct for the wipe and the tagline. The arrow's `to` is invisible, which is
   also correct. But a 0ms animation does not always commit in every engine, so
   the resting state is asserted outright for the two that must be visible. */
:root[data-motion='off'] .wm-enter { clip-path: none; }
:root[data-motion='off'] .wm-enter-tag { opacity: 1; }
:root[data-motion='off'] .wm-enter .splash-text-v::after,
:root[data-motion='off'] .wm-enter .sidebar-wordmark-v::after,
:root[data-motion='off'] .wm-enter .auth-wordmark-v::after { display: none; }

@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  :root:not([data-motion='full']) .wm-enter { clip-path: none; }
  :root:not([data-motion='full']) .wm-enter-tag { opacity: 1; }
  :root:not([data-motion='full']) .wm-enter .splash-text-v::after,
  :root:not([data-motion='full']) .wm-enter .sidebar-wordmark-v::after,
  :root:not([data-motion='full']) .wm-enter .auth-wordmark-v::after { display: none; }
}

/* ---------- Moment 1: a bill was created ---------- */

/* The receipt earns the biggest gesture in the app — it is the thing the shopkeeper
   does two hundred times a day, and the one moment worth making feel good. */
.moment-receipt {
  animation: receipt-in calc(var(--motion-slow) * var(--motion-scale)) var(--motion-spring) both;
}

@keyframes receipt-in {
  from { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(14px) scale(0.97); }
  to { opacity: 1; transform: none; }
}

/* A single ring that expands and fades — cheap (one element, transform + opacity only,
   both GPU-composited) where a particle burst would cost a canvas and a frame budget. */
.moment-burst {
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;
  pointer-events: none;
  border-radius: inherit;
  animation: burst-ring calc(var(--motion-slow) * var(--motion-scale)) var(--motion-ease) both;
}

@keyframes burst-ring {
  0% { box-shadow: 0 0 0 0 rgba(var(--brand-rgb), 0.5); opacity: 1; }
  100% { box-shadow: 0 0 0 22px rgba(var(--brand-rgb), 0); opacity: 0; }
}

.moment-tick {
  animation: tick-pop calc(var(--motion-base) * var(--motion-scale)) var(--motion-spring) both;
}

@keyframes tick-pop {
  from { transform: scale(0.4); opacity: 0; }
  to { transform: scale(1); opacity: 1; }
}

/* ---------- Moment 2: money arrived ---------- */

/* Green, rising, brief. Deliberately a different colour and direction from the bill
   moment so the two never read as the same event out of the corner of an eye. */
.moment-money {
  animation: money-in calc(var(--motion-slow) * var(--motion-scale)) var(--motion-spring) both;
}

@keyframes money-in {
  0% { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(10px); }
  60% { opacity: 1; transform: translateY(-2px); }
  100% { opacity: 1; transform: none; }
}

.moment-money-glow::after {
  content: '';
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;
  border-radius: inherit;
  pointer-events: none;
  animation: money-glow calc(var(--motion-slow) * var(--motion-scale)) var(--motion-ease) both;
}

@keyframes money-glow {
  0% { box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 2px rgba(52, 211, 153, 0.55); }
  100% { box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 2px rgba(52, 211, 153, 0); }
}

/* ---------- Moment 3: something needs attention ---------- */

/* Three pulses then stop. An indefinite pulse becomes wallpaper within a day and stops
   being read as urgent at all — which is the opposite of what an alert is for. */
.moment-alert {
  animation: alert-pulse calc(1100ms * var(--motion-scale)) var(--motion-ease) 3;
}

@keyframes alert-pulse {
  0%, 100% { box-shadow: 0 0 0 0 rgba(248, 113, 113, 0); }
  50% { box-shadow: 0 0 0 5px rgba(248, 113, 113, 0.22); }
}

/* ---------- Moment 4: a new order landed ---------- */

.moment-arrive {
  animation: order-arrive calc(var(--motion-slow) * var(--motion-scale)) var(--motion-spring) both;
}

@keyframes order-arrive {
  from { opacity: 0; transform: translateX(-16px); }
  to { opacity: 1; transform: none; }
}

/* Rides on the existing .badge/.chip; marks the row as new until it is looked at. */
.moment-new-flag {
  animation: new-flag calc(1400ms * var(--motion-scale)) var(--motion-ease) 3;
}

@keyframes new-flag {
  0%, 100% { background: rgba(var(--brand-rgb), 0.1); }
  50% { background: rgba(var(--brand-rgb), 0.26); }
}

/* ---------- Moment 5: progress against the day ---------- */

/* Fills from 0 to whatever inline --fill the component sets, so the number is earned
   on screen rather than just appearing. */
.moment-fill > i {
  display: block;
  height: 100%;
  border-radius: inherit;
  background: linear-gradient(90deg, var(--brand-dark), var(--brand));
  width: var(--fill, 0%);
  animation: fill-grow calc(var(--motion-slow) * var(--motion-scale)) var(--motion-ease) both;
}

@keyframes fill-grow {
  from { width: 0%; }
  to { width: var(--fill, 0%); }
}

/* ---------- Ambient (decorative) ----------
   Only ever rendered on login/signup and empty states. Anything using .fx-ambient is
   removed outright at reduced/off, so it must never carry meaning. */

.fx-ambient {
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;
  overflow: hidden;
  pointer-events: none;
  z-index: 0;
  border-radius: inherit;
}

.fx-ambient span {
  position: absolute;
  border-radius: 50%;
  filter: blur(42px);
  opacity: 0.5;
  will-change: transform;
}

.fx-ambient span:nth-child(1) {
  width: 46%; padding-bottom: 46%; left: -8%; top: -14%;
  background: rgba(var(--brand-rgb), 0.55);
  animation: drift-a calc(19s / max(var(--motion-scale), 0.001)) ease-in-out infinite;
}

.fx-ambient span:nth-child(2) {
  width: 38%; padding-bottom: 38%; right: -6%; top: 22%;
  background: var(--gold);
  opacity: 0.28;
  animation: drift-b calc(23s / max(var(--motion-scale), 0.001)) ease-in-out infinite;
}

.fx-ambient span:nth-child(3) {
  width: 42%; padding-bottom: 42%; left: 24%; bottom: -20%;
  background: rgba(var(--brand-rgb), 0.35);
  animation: drift-c calc(27s / max(var(--motion-scale), 0.001)) ease-in-out infinite;
}

@keyframes drift-a {
  0%, 100% { transform: translate3d(0, 0, 0) scale(1); }
  50% { transform: translate3d(14%, 10%, 0) scale(1.12); }
}

@keyframes drift-b {
  0%, 100% { transform: translate3d(0, 0, 0) scale(1); }
  50% { transform: translate3d(-12%, 14%, 0) scale(0.9); }
}

@keyframes drift-c {
  0%, 100% { transform: translate3d(0, 0, 0) scale(1); }
  50% { transform: translate3d(10%, -12%, 0) scale(1.08); }
}

/* =========================================================================
   Theme picker — the swatch grid in Settings › Look & feel
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Each card previews its OWN palette, so it cannot use var(--brand): the
   component passes the theme's colours in as --sw-brand / --sw-rgb inline.
   That is also what makes the selected card glow in its own colour instead
   of the currently-active one.
   ========================================================================= */

/* A row of swatches, not a grid of cards. Eleven 196x74px cards spent roughly
   350px of the settings page on one preference, in three rows with a ragged
   last one. At 150px they wrap into two tidy rows and the whole control is
   about 90px — which is the size a colour picker should be. */
.theme-picker {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(150px, 1fr));
  gap: 0.4rem;
}

.theme-swatch {
  position: relative;
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 0.55rem;
  padding: 0.4rem 0.6rem;
  border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
  border: 1px solid var(--card-border);
  background: var(--surface-card);
  box-shadow: var(--card-shadow), var(--card-hairline);
  color: var(--text);
  font: inherit;
  text-align: left;
  cursor: pointer;
  transition: border-color 0.18s ease, box-shadow 0.18s ease, transform 0.18s ease;
}

@media (hover: hover) {
  .theme-swatch:hover {
    border-color: rgba(var(--sw-rgb), 0.55);
    transform: translateY(-2px);
    /* Neutral elevation, same as every other card lifting off the page — not a
       coloured glow. See the note on .theme-swatch.active just below for why a
       tinted throw shadow was tried here before and taken back out. */
    box-shadow: var(--card-shadow-hover), var(--card-hairline);
  }
}

.theme-swatch:focus-visible {
  outline: none;
  box-shadow: 0 0 0 3px rgba(var(--sw-rgb), 0.35);
}

/* A ring, not a glow. The coloured throw shadow made the chosen swatch float off
   the page like a notification, and eleven of them meant one card was always
   casting light on its neighbours. */
.theme-swatch.active {
  border-color: var(--sw-brand);
  box-shadow: var(--card-shadow), var(--card-hairline), inset 0 0 0 1px var(--sw-brand);
}

/* Three tones stacked into one pill — brand, its dark edge, its light tint. Reads as
   a palette at a glance, which a single flat dot does not. */
/* ONE solid dot of the accent, not the accent's palette.

   This was a 40px circle sliced into three vertical stripes — brand, brandDark,
   brandLight — which is the shop's internal ramp put on display. At 40px those
   slices do not read as "a colour with tones", they read as a pie chart, and
   eleven of them down a settings page look like a chart legend. Nobody choosing
   their shop's colour needs to see the derived tones; they need to see the
   colour. Every tool that ships an accent picker — Linear, Slack, Notion,
   Figma — shows one swatch, and that is the whole reason those pickers look
   like software and this one did not.

   The two extra <span>s are still emitted by ThemePicker.js and are simply not
   painted, so the component keeps working untouched. */
.theme-swatch-tones {
  display: block;
  flex-shrink: 0;
  width: 22px;
  height: 22px;
  border-radius: var(--radius-full);
  overflow: hidden;
  background: var(--sw-brand);
  box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.22);
}

.theme-swatch-tones span { display: none; }



.theme-swatch-meta {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  gap: 0.1rem;
  min-width: 0;
}

.theme-swatch-meta strong {
  font-size: var(--fs-sm);
  font-weight: 600;
}

/* The hint is gone from the swatch. "Genda — default", "Neel — deep blue" — a
   sentence describing a colour, beside the colour, which is the one thing on the
   page that needs no description. It was also too long for the card and shipped
   truncated ("Navy — the house c…", "Monochrome — mo…"), which reads as an
   unfinished screen. It stays in lib/themes.js and in each button's aria-label,
   so a screen reader still hears it. */
.theme-swatch-meta small { display: none; }

.theme-swatch-meta small {
  font-size: var(--fs-xs);
  color: var(--text-muted);
  white-space: nowrap;
  overflow: hidden;
  text-overflow: ellipsis;
}

.theme-swatch-tick {
  position: absolute;
  top: 0.5rem;
  right: 0.5rem;
  width: 15px;
  height: 15px;
  color: var(--sw-brand);
}

.theme-picker-error {
  grid-column: 1 / -1;
  margin: 0.2rem 0 0;
  font-size: var(--fs-sm);
  color: var(--text-danger);
}

/* =========================================================================
   SCENE PICKER  (Settings -> App ka look)
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Sits directly above the colour swatches, because a scene is the bigger of
   the two choices and the colour is chosen INSIDE it.

   Each option is a 96x60 picture of a screen — page ground, rail, one card,
   one short card — built from four inline custom properties the component
   passes in from lib/scenes.js. A picture rather than colour chips because
   what separates these five is not hue: it is whether the card is glass or
   paper, how hard its edge is, and how much light is in the room. Three dots
   would make Aurora and Mehfil look like the same swatch in two colours.

   Everything paints from --sh-*, never from var(--brand) or var(--surface),
   so a swatch shows its OWN scene rather than the one currently applied.
   ========================================================================= */

/* 232px produced a 3 + 2 grid with a card-and-a-half hole in the second row.
   There are exactly five scenes and there will always be few, so the grid is told
   to draw five columns once the settings column is wide enough, instead of being
   left to auto-fill into a 4 + 1 with a hole where the fifth should be. */
.scene-picker {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(150px, 1fr));
  gap: 0.5rem;
  margin-bottom: 1.1rem;
}

@media (min-width: 900px) {
  .scene-picker { grid-template-columns: repeat(5, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
}

/* Stacked, not side by side. Beside a 96px preview the text column was ~60px
   wide, so "Glass over northern light" broke over three lines and every card in
   the row ended up a different height — a grid of five uneven boxes, which is the
   thing that makes a picker look unfinished. Preview on top at full card width,
   name and description under it: the same shape every operating system uses for
   an appearance picker, and the preview gets bigger rather than smaller. */
.scene-swatch {
  position: relative;
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  align-items: stretch;
  gap: 0.5rem;
  padding: 0.5rem;
  border-radius: var(--radius-md);
  border: 1px solid var(--border);
  background: var(--surface);
  color: var(--text);
  font: inherit;
  text-align: left;
  cursor: pointer;
  transition: border-color 0.18s ease, box-shadow 0.18s ease, transform 0.18s ease;
}

@media (hover: hover) {
  .scene-swatch:hover {
    border-color: var(--border-strong);
    transform: translateY(-1px);
  }
}

.scene-swatch:focus-visible {
  outline: none;
  box-shadow: var(--focus-ring);
}

/* A ring, matching the colour swatches. The coloured throw shadow made the chosen
   scene glow like a notification on a page whose whole job is showing you five
   calm previews side by side. */
.scene-swatch.active {
  border-color: var(--brand);
  box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1px var(--brand);
}

/* The little screen. `overflow: hidden` is what lets the rail and cards be
   positioned past the rounded corner without poking out of it. */
.scene-shot {
  position: relative;
  flex-shrink: 0;
  width: 100%;
  height: 76px;
  border-radius: 6px;
  overflow: hidden;
  /* Two corner lights over the page, because that is what actually separates one
     scene from another now that the rail follows the page in both modes. One wash
     could not tell Aurora's blue-and-teal night from Mehfil's violet-and-gold
     room; Noir and Classic pass `transparent` for the second and stay flat, which
     is exactly what those two are. */
  background:
    radial-gradient(80% 110% at 6% -18%, var(--sh-wash), transparent 66%),
    radial-gradient(75% 100% at 100% 108%, var(--sh-wash2, transparent), transparent 64%),
    var(--sh-page);
  box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1px var(--sh-edge);
}

/* The rail sits a few percent off the page in both modes now, so it needs its own
   edge or it disappears entirely on a light scene — and "there is a sidebar" is
   still true and still worth showing. */
.scene-shot-rail {
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0 auto 0 0;
  width: 24px;
  background: var(--sh-rail);
  box-shadow: inset -1px 0 0 var(--sh-edge);
}

.scene-shot-card {
  position: absolute;
  left: 30px;
  right: 8px;
  height: 17px;
  border-radius: 4px;
  background: var(--sh-card);
  box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1px var(--sh-edge);
  top: 9px;
}

.scene-shot-card.is-short {
  top: 31px;
  height: 20px;
}

.scene-swatch-meta {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  gap: 0.12rem;
  min-width: 0;
}

.scene-swatch-meta {
  padding: 0 0.15rem 0.15rem;
}

.scene-swatch-meta strong {
  font-size: var(--fs-sm);
  font-weight: 600;
}

/* Wraps to two lines rather than ellipsing, unlike the colour swatch's hint.
   These hints are a real sentence about what the scene is FOR ("best in
   daylight"), which is the only thing that helps someone choose between five
   looks they cannot see at once — clipping it would throw away the useful half. */
/* Clamped to three lines. These descriptions run from four words to twenty, so
   left to themselves the five cards in the row end up four different heights and
   the picker looks broken. Three lines fits the longest of them at this width and
   every card in the row is the same box. */
.scene-swatch-meta small {
  display: -webkit-box;
  -webkit-line-clamp: 3;
  line-clamp: 3;
  -webkit-box-orient: vertical;
  overflow: hidden;
  font-size: var(--fs-2xs);
  color: var(--text-faint);
  line-height: 1.35;
}

/* The row is a grid, so stretching each button makes every preview and every
   label land on the same line across all five. */
.scene-swatch { height: 100%; }

.scene-swatch-tick {
  position: absolute;
  top: 0.5rem;
  right: 0.5rem;
  width: 15px;
  height: 15px;
  color: var(--brand-text);
}

.scene-picker-error {
  grid-column: 1 / -1;
  margin: 0.2rem 0 0;
  font-size: var(--fs-sm);
  color: var(--text-danger);
}

/* ---------- Day progress (moment 5) ----------
   Lived in the greeting hero until 2026-09-03, where it was a 200px sliver at the
   far right of a page header. It is a statement about the day's takings and it now
   sits at the foot of the Munafa card, under the figure it qualifies — so the
   class name says `day-` and not `greet-`. */

.day-progress {
  margin-top: 0.7rem;
  max-width: 340px;
}

.day-progress-line {
  display: flex;
  align-items: baseline;
  justify-content: space-between;
  gap: 0.6rem;
  font-size: var(--fs-xs);
  color: var(--text-muted);
  margin-bottom: 0.3rem;
}

.day-progress-line > span:first-child {
  font-weight: 700;
  color: var(--text);
}

.day-progress-ahead { color: var(--text-success); }

.day-progress-ref { flex-shrink: 0; }

.day-progress-bar {
  height: 6px;
  border-radius: var(--radius-full);
  background: rgba(var(--brand-rgb), 0.14);
  overflow: hidden;
}

/* ---------- Motion level picker ---------- */

/* Settings pickers: a row of labelled cards, one of them active. Shared by the
   animation picker and the screen-fit (display density) picker — they are the same
   control with different options, so they read as a matched pair on the page.
   `.density-option` here is the app-wide display setting; the unrelated
   `.density-toggle` above is the two-icon row-height control on a single table. */
.motion-picker,
/* Mode, motion, text size and density ALL render through .density-picker — four
   settings x three options = twelve full-width cards stacked down one panel, each
   with a bold label and a sentence under it. A choice between three named options
   is a segmented control; it is the most standard control in back-office software
   and it takes one row instead of three.

   auto-fit at a 150px floor is what produced the stack: in a settings column the
   tracks grew to ~390px each. Sized to their content now and aligned left, so
   three short options read as one control rather than as three cards that happen
   to sit next to each other. */
.density-picker {
  display: inline-flex;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  gap: 0.35rem;
}

.motion-option,
.density-option {
  display: flex;
  align-items: baseline;
  gap: 0.4rem;
  min-height: 34px;
  padding: 0.35rem 0.7rem;
  border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
  border: 1px solid var(--border);
  background: var(--surface);
  color: var(--text-muted);
  font: inherit;
  text-align: left;
  cursor: pointer;
  transition: border-color 0.15s ease, background 0.15s ease, color 0.15s ease;
}

.motion-option strong,
.density-option strong { font-size: var(--fs-sm); font-weight: 600; }
/* The hint sits on the same line as its label now, and quieter — it is a gloss on
   the word, not a second line of equal weight. */
.motion-option small,
.density-option small { font-size: var(--fs-2xs); color: var(--text-faint); }

/* Selected reads by ink and edge. A brand-tinted fill on the chosen option, four
   times down one panel, put more colour on "which text size am I on" than this
   page spends on anything that matters. */
.motion-option.active,
.density-option.active {
  border-color: var(--border-strong);
  background: var(--surface-strong);
  color: var(--text);
}

.motion-option:focus-visible,
.density-option:focus-visible {
  outline: none;
  box-shadow: 0 0 0 3px rgba(var(--brand-rgb), 0.3);
}

/* Screen fit is a desktop affordance — the same call the table density toggle
   already makes. Below 901px the layout is a drawer plus cards with its own
   tuning and the setting changes nothing, so offering it would be a control
   that visibly does not work. */
@media (max-width: 900px) {
  .density-setting {
    display: none;
  }
}

/* ==========================================================================
   Responsive hardening
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   One place that answers "does this fit?" for each size class the dashboard
   actually ships to. Nothing new is declared here — every rule adjusts width
   or spacing on vocabulary already defined above, so a page composed from that
   vocabulary picks these up without touching the page itself.

   Size classes, and what each one is solving:

     ≤ 400px   small Android phone — the shopkeeper's own handset. Gutters are
               pulled in because at the default padding a stat card had barely
               half its width left for the number itself.
     ≤ 900px   phone + tablet portrait. Handled by the off-canvas drawer block
               near the top of this file; nothing repeated here.
     901–1280  tablet landscape and laptop. The sidebar is still permanent, so
               the content column is ~232px narrower than the window — the page
               gutter shrinks to hand that back to the wide tables.
     ≥ 1281    desktop. .content-area is centred (see its own rule), so the
               dashboard sits in the middle of the screen instead of hugging
               the sidebar with a third of the monitor left empty.

   The existing @media blocks above are deliberately left alone; because these
   rules come later in the file they must never re-open a range those blocks
   already own, which is why the laptop band below is bounded on BOTH sides.
   ========================================================================== */

@media (min-width: 901px) and (max-width: 1280px) {
  .content-area {
    padding-left: 1.4rem;
    padding-right: 1.4rem;
  }
}

@media (max-width: 400px) {
  .content-area {
    padding-left: 0.75rem;
    padding-right: 0.75rem;
  }

  /* The topbar carries a search field plus up to five icon buttons. Below this width
     they can't always share one line without the search box collapsing to a stub, so
     the row is allowed to wrap.

     `flex: 1 1 100%` on the search used to force that wrap unconditionally, which was
     too blunt: a plain seller shows three icon buttons (calculator, theme, bell), and
     three 40px buttons plus a 150px search fit a 360px handset with room to spare. The
     result was a second row holding three buttons against the left edge and 200px of
     nothing — two rows of chrome, mostly empty, above the actual page.

     A flex-basis floor instead of a full-width claim lets the row wrap only when it
     genuinely cannot fit, which is what happens once the attendance and impersonation
     buttons join in. 150px is the width at which the search still shows a few words of
     what was typed rather than becoming the stub the note above worried about. */
  .quick-topbar {
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    /* Only has an effect on a row that DID wrap: on an unwrapped row the search is
       flex: 1 and absorbs every spare pixel, so there is no free space to distribute.
       When it does wrap, this puts the buttons under the right-hand end of the search
       box instead of stranding them at the far left. */
    justify-content: flex-end;
  }

  .quick-topbar .search-wrap {
    flex: 1 1 150px;
  }

  /* Login / signup / reset. At the default 1.5rem shell + 2rem card padding a 360px
     handset had 248px left for the form itself, which is where the signup wizard's
     step labels started wrapping. */
  .auth-shell {
    padding: 1rem;
  }

  .auth-card {
    padding: 1.35rem 1.15rem;
  }

  .panel {
    padding: 1.1rem 0.9rem;
  }

  .greet-hero {
    padding: 1.2rem 1rem;
  }

  .stat-card {
    padding: 0.95rem 0.9rem;
  }

  .stat-grid {
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(118px, 1fr));
  }

  .data-panel-body {
    padding: 0.9rem 0.75rem;
  }

  .data-filters,
  .data-panel-head,
  .pagination,
  .bulk-bar,
  .record-card,
  .disclosure-trigger {
    padding-left: 0.75rem;
    padding-right: 0.75rem;
  }

  .data-table th:first-child,
  .data-table td:first-child { padding-left: 0.75rem; }
  .data-table th:last-child,
  .data-table td:last-child { padding-right: 0.75rem; }

  .modal-overlay {
    --ov-top: 0.6rem;
    --ov-bottom: calc(0.6rem + env(safe-area-inset-bottom, 0px));
    padding: var(--ov-top) 0.6rem var(--ov-bottom);
  }

  .modal-card {
    padding: 1rem 0.9rem;
  }
}

/* The bell panel is anchored to the right edge of a button that already sits at the
   right edge of the screen, so its own width is the only thing keeping it on-screen.
   That assumption holds on desktop and is FALSE on a phone — see the fix below, which
   takes the panel out of the button's coordinate space entirely at those widths. */
.notif-dropdown {
  max-width: min(320px, calc(100vw - 1.75rem));
}

/* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   The notification panel on a phone
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   The panel is `position: absolute; right: 0; left: auto; width: 320px` inside
   `.bell-wrap`, which is `position: relative` — so it hangs 320px LEFTWARDS from
   the bell. The comment above spells out the assumption that makes that safe:
   the bell sits at the right edge of the screen, so leftwards is into the page.

   Below 401px that stops being true. `.quick-topbar` wraps there (the search box
   takes the whole first line and the icon buttons drop to a second row), so the
   bell moves from the right edge to roughly 110px from the LEFT edge — and a
   320px panel hanging leftwards from there starts at about x = -170. Which is
   exactly what it did: a white slab bleeding off the left of the screen with the
   product names and the "expired — clear them off" headline cut in half.
   `max-width` could never have saved it; the panel was never too wide, it was
   anchored to the wrong edge.

   The fix is to stop measuring from the bell at all. Making `.bell-wrap` static
   hands the panel's coordinate space to `.quick-topbar`, so `left: 0; right: 0`
   spans the topbar — which on a phone is the content column. `top: 100%` then
   means "under the whole topbar, both rows", not "under the bell".

   Full-width is also simply the right shape here: a 320px card floating under one
   icon is a desktop idiom, and a phone reads a list of the day's alerts far better
   edge to edge. Scoped to 640px rather than the 400px where the wrap happens,
   because the leftwards hang is fragile at any width where the bell might not be
   the last control in the row, and this removes the fragility instead of moving it.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
@media (max-width: 640px) {
  .bell-wrap {
    position: static;
  }

  .quick-topbar {
    position: relative;
  }

  .notif-dropdown {
    left: 0;
    right: 0;
    width: auto;
    max-width: none;
    /* The panel is now as tall as it is wide-reaching, so give it more of the
       screen than the desktop 360px before it starts scrolling internally. */
    max-height: min(60vh, 60dvh);
  }
}

/* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Sideways scrollers must not reach the browser's back gesture

   Android Chrome and iOS Safari navigate back when a horizontal swipe runs off
   the end of the page. Any element that scrolls sideways hands the gesture on
   once it hits its own edge, so swiping through a wide table, a row of filter
   chips or a payment-mode strip could quietly leave the screen — taking a
   half-filled bill or purchase order with it, since none of these forms are
   drafts until they are saved.

   `contain` stops the chaining at the element without disabling anything: the
   scroller still scrolls, the page still scrolls, only the escape to browser
   navigation is cut. Declared on X alone so vertical scrolling is untouched —
   see .table-wrap, where containing Y was itself the bug.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
.panel,
.chip-row,
.segmented,
.range-chips,
.invoice-stage,
.plan-compare-wrap,
.suggestion-row {
  overscroll-behavior-x: contain;
}

/* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Label-and-value rows

   Every row listed here is the same shape: a translated label on the left, a
   number or a status on the right, laid out with flex. A flex item's default
   min-width is `auto` — it refuses to shrink below its own content — so the
   row cannot get narrower than label + value, whatever box it is in. Two real
   inputs break that assumption every day:

     · names. "Ramesh Kumar Patil" is a normal khata customer.
     · translation. The same label in Marathi, Tamil or Malayalam runs 30-60%
       longer than the English these rows were measured against.

   When it happens the row grows past its panel and .panel's overflow-x: auto
   catches it — that's the stray horizontal scrollbar this app has been chasing
   (see the billing overflow fix). min-width: 0 lets the label shrink and wrap
   instead, which is what it should have done all along.

   Only the text side is unpinned. The value keeps its natural width, and the
   big money figures below are explicitly held on one line — a rupee amount
   broken across two lines is worse than a narrow label.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
.notif-item > *,
.notif-detail-row > *,
.module-row > *,
.activity-row > *,
.reco-item > *,
.disclosure-trigger > *,
.cart-total-bar > *,
.customer-hero-left > *,
.line-items-head > *,
.data-panel-head > * {
  min-width: 0;
}

/* Icons and avatars in those rows are sized, not text — they must not be shrunk
   by the rule above. Most already say so; this covers the rest. */
.notif-item > svg,
.activity-row > svg,
.reco-item > svg,
.disclosure-trigger > svg,
.customer-hero-left > img,
.customer-hero-left > .avatar-initials {
  flex-shrink: 0;
}

.cart-total-value,
.customer-hero-amount,
.line-total strong,
.order-totals strong,
.statement-totals strong {
  white-space: nowrap;
}

/* The billing cart's phone card list. .record-card was sized for the flush-edge
   .data-panel frame; the cart sits inside a .panel that already has its own gutter, so
   the side padding is dropped rather than doubled. */
/* (`.cart-card` lived here for the billing cart's phone-only `.record-card` copy. The
   cart is one `.cart-lines` grid at every width now, so there is no second markup to
   style — see the cart line list block near the end of this file.) */

/* An online order card has no side column — the amount rides in the meta row with the
   status badges, and Accept/Reject sit full-width at the bottom where a thumb is. */
.order-card .row-actions {
  margin-top: 0.5rem;
}

.order-card .row-actions .btn {
  flex: 1 1 auto;
}

.order-card .order-items-line {
  max-width: none;
}

